WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.197
Welcome to Module Eight, Lesson One: observability for agents.

2
00:00:04.527 --> 00:00:07.207
Watching an agent takes more than watching a server.

3
00:00:08.007 --> 00:00:12.544
Picture HomeShield, an insurer whose agent helps customers file home claims.

4
00:00:12.874 --> 00:00:16.749
One day, a simple claim takes nine seconds and still gets it wrong.

5
00:00:17.549 --> 00:00:20.337
Old monitoring can only say the request was slow.

6
00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:24.596
An agent trace says why: the planner chose the wrong tool twice,

7
00:00:24.646 --> 00:00:26.462
and retrieval came back empty.

8
00:00:27.262 --> 00:00:31.287
So make every model call, tool call, retrieval, and handoff a span.

9
00:00:31.617 --> 00:00:36.132
Each one records the model and prompt version, tokens, latency, and cost.

10
00:00:36.932 --> 00:00:41.277
It also records the tool's name and arguments, the result, and retries.

11
00:00:41.607 --> 00:00:43.989
Run and session IDs tie it all together.

12
00:00:44.789 --> 00:00:46.104
Those arguments matter.

13
00:00:46.434 --> 00:00:50.331
Without them, you can't tell a wrong tool from the right tool used badly.

14
00:00:51.131 --> 00:00:56.435
Use OpenTelemetry, the open standard that NeMo Agent Toolkit emits natively.

15
00:00:56.765 --> 00:01:00.005
Instrument once, and send the same spans to Phoenix,

16
00:01:00.055 --> 00:01:02.155
Langfuse, Datadog, or any backend.

17
00:01:02.955 --> 00:01:05.103
But full traces hold customer data.

18
00:01:05.433 --> 00:01:07.859
Redact it before export, limit retention,

19
00:01:07.909 --> 00:01:11.121
and guard trace storage like your production database.

20
00:01:11.921 --> 00:01:14.026
Next, pick the signals worth an alert.

21
00:01:14.356 --> 00:01:16.674
Task success rate reflects user value,

22
00:01:16.724 --> 00:01:19.853
and tool errors belong per tool, not just in total.

23
00:01:20.653 --> 00:01:24.189
Rising steps per task, or more runs hitting their budget cap,

24
00:01:24.239 --> 00:01:25.893
mean the agent is struggling.

25
00:01:26.223 --> 00:01:28.511
More escalations mean lost capability,

26
00:01:28.561 --> 00:01:31.464
and falling groundedness warns of hallucination.

27
00:01:32.264 --> 00:01:34.902
A spike in one guardrail can signal an attack.

28
00:01:35.232 --> 00:01:39.705
And p95 latency and cost per resolved task are your two targets.

29
00:01:40.505 --> 00:01:41.756
Then capture feedback.

30
00:01:42.086 --> 00:01:43.677
Thumbs are precise but rare,

31
00:01:43.727 --> 00:01:47.604
while rephrasing or giving up are quiet signals you get constantly.

32
00:01:48.404 --> 00:01:51.959
Tie each signal to its trace ID and feed the data flywheel.

33
00:01:52.289 --> 00:01:54.415
That's how monitoring becomes improvement.

34
00:01:55.215 --> 00:01:58.472
HomeShield once tried a cheaper model to cut cost per token.

35
00:01:58.802 --> 00:02:02.763
It failed more and took more steps, so each resolved claim cost more.

36
00:02:03.563 --> 00:02:06.765
On the exam, when asked what to optimise or report,

37
00:02:06.815 --> 00:02:09.507
choose cost per successfully resolved task.

38
00:02:09.837 --> 00:02:12.241
Now read on to see what every span should carry.
